    <TIER LINGUISTIC_TYPE_REF="default-lt" TIER_ID="default"/>
    <TIER LINGUISTIC_TYPE_REF="imported-sep" TIER_ID="SPEAKER_00">
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a171"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts8" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts10">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>[music]</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a5"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts26" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts28">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>I'm great. Great to be here, Alex.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a7"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts34" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts36">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yeah, some of the leading experts actually suggest that we are experiencing an epidemic of loneliness in the United States and that disconnection, rise of social media, and a decrease of embodied conversation has something to do with that. Also is increasing communication anxiety.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a8"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts38" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts40">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Third of all, people are losing the ability to empathically connect with one another, to actually be able to do perspective taking and see other people's points of view.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a11"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts50" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts52">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yeah. You know, one of the things that you pointed to, Alex, is just this decrease in practice in having face-to-face conversations. You know, when you and I were young, we had the family phone, and even these sort of inane small conversations of, hello, Tracy residents, this is Sarah speaking, and making small talk with our parents' friends, my brother's friends, et cetera, gave us that daily practice. And people are wearing more and more earpods, looking at their phones and so on. So whereas we might have passed the time in an airport or in public transportation having these conversations with strangers, we're more likely to be engrossed in our own mediated communication with people we already know or connected to.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a14"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts62" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts64">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Well, people go to social media intending to connect. However, the research shows that we actually feel worse after being on social media. The average per day, by the way, two and a half hours a day that we spend on social media. So obviously we're craving that connection. But the thing is, on social media, we're more likely to come across these sort of photoshopped pictures. I call it photoshopped lives. Some people have called it curated cells, like we curate a museum, we curate our brand as a person. And so this ends up leaving us feeling like our lives in real life are not as great as our friends' life. And this social comparison is a happiness killer.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a16"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts70" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts72">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yeah, the research actually shows that people use the emojis for negative emotions hardly at all compared to the positive emotions. And so one of the downsides of that too is we really don't see when other people are suffering. And we are most likely to reach out and connect to someone when we see that they are suffering. So it's almost like the suffering becomes invisible and then that fuels the disconnection between people.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a18"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts78" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts80">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yeah, well, embodied what I mean by that is that you have access to a number of different nonverbal cues. So ideally, right, that's face-to-face. So we're with our family, friends, we hear the burps, the farts, the giggles, the, you know, the dirty diaper, the smell of the great food being made, and so on. And that gives us a sense of connection. But even when it cannot be face-to-face, it can still be embodied in some ways. Like you and I, we're able to see each other right now. I'm able to see if I say something and it doesn't hit exactly right by maybe a tone of or a facial expression. And even telephone, we know that the way somebody says something affects how we understand the message. And so knowing the tone of voice, is this sarcasm? Is the person really excited? Et cetera.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a20"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts86" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts88">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Mm-hmm. Well, there's a couple of reasons. First of all, if we are at all suffering, upset, or angry, there are likely to be a lot fewer misunderstandings when we have access to multiple nonverbal paravocalic cues. So that's one thing. The second thing is that the happiest people in the world are those that have the most social connections. And so when we go running to our phone or social media intending to think we're going to get that social connection, we could be better spending that time and we get more of a happiness boost if we were with people face to face. And when we see them face to face, we realize that they are whole human beings and they may have beautiful aspects to their life, but they're also suffering. And so we're less likely to do that social comparison as well.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a22"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts94" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts96">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Right, it goes from beyond dyadic and like, you know, these two little information things to this whole relationship, which includes a lot of people.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a24"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts102" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts104">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yeah, so whenever someone is suffering or upset, embodied conversations are much better than mediated conversations. And that's because lots of times we don't know what to say, but even a facial expression or a hand on a shoulder or a pat on the back or a hug is going to show that we are with the other person. We acknowledge their suffering. Or if somebody is upset or angry, we can show that, oh gosh, I got it. Like, you know, goodness, I got that you're angry. Because when people are upset, first of all, they need to feel acknowledged before they're going to take any information or advice and be willing to solve the problem.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a26"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts108" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts112">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>And so trying to mediate conflict over medi trying to mediate conflict over mediated communication is a recipe for d disaster.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a29"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts120" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts124">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yeah, these little micro expressions people are able to provide.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a31"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts130" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts132">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yeah, yeah, definitely. I'm not saying that everything should be solved by embodied conversation. And whenever really the point of a communication is to deliver information, mediated communication is fantastic and can be much more efficient. In fact, having meetings, for instance, that where everyone is together and it's just one report after another report.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a168"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts138" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts141">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>[laugh]</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a33"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts144" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts146">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yes, yes, so</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a35"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts150" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts154">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yeah, so if you need to make decisions and collaborate, meetings are great ways to do that. But just information, email is fantastic.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a37"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts160" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts162">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yeah, well, more and more companies are realizing that their first thoughts of like, oh, we're going to make everything efficient through telecommuting and emails and so on, that it really does not always solve the problem. And so some companies are really going in a different direction, encouraging more people to come into work and designing what's called dialogic spaces. And dialogic spaces are those spaces where people that are specifically designed for people to come in embodied space with one another and create relationship. And that could happen in a ton of different ways. Some people are doing these sort of stand-up huddle desk for meetings where you don't sit down, you don't have enough room to have all your media out, cafeterias where people have to stand in line long enough to bump shoulders and engage in small talk, but not long enough maybe for them to pull out their phones. Agenda-less breakfast meetings, interactive company outings. You know, at ASU, in terms of our mentoring, we actually have this activity that we call walk and talk. And it's a space where there's no agenda, but faculty and graduate students get together and go on a walk, hike, and talk. And so many creative ideas come out of that and also a building of community and morale.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a191"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts169" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts170">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Right, like forced family fun time, right?</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a158"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts171" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts172">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Go have some fun to gether</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a40"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts173" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts174">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yes, but in some ways. You know, like they're definitely encouraged, but just designing for conversation does not automatically equate with this interaction. There's going to be some people that still think, oh, the real work is me sitting down at my desk and returning emails. And as such, it's really imperative that.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a41"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts175" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts176">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Bosses, leaders embody themselves this sort of conversation. For instance, one idea would be having an hour a week where you put a sign on your door that says free listening. And free listening is someone comes in and doesn't have to have an agenda, and it's just listening in.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a44"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts186" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts188">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yeah. Yeah. You know, one of the things is just realizing that putting down our social media is hard. It is difficult because in many ways our social media is like a drug. Every time we get that buzzer beep, we get a quick dopamine hit. And it's almost like the gambler who gets that dopamine hit and you think like, oh my gosh, this email I'm getting, it might finally be that email I was waiting for to tell me I got this big award.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a46"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts194" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts196">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yeah, yeah, and usually it's what, another spam risk or something like this.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a47"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts198" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts200">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>And, but that dopamine hit is actually much different than the longer-lasting serotonin lift we get from social connections. But we've kind of got to go over that hump of like, okay, this is going to feel difficult in the short run, but it is actually better for my well-being. So that's one thing, putting down our social media. And then also, just even if it's a few times a week or just starting small, choosing those video and phone conversations rather than the mediated connection. So if you have the choice between ordering pizza online versus calling, if you don't have a lot of practice, practice in low-risk situations. I like to also talk to strangers, and strangers, talking to strangers actually gives us a happiness lift. One way I do this is in the elevator at school. And, you know, oftentimes people have their ears with the ear pods and looking at their screen. And we may talk about something very inane, you know, like are those Cheetos or Dorits on the floor of the</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a48"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts202" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts204">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Elevator. However, we end up leaving with a smile and kind of a connection. And oftentimes, people are just pleasantly surprised, you know, that somebody would be interested in chatting with them.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a161"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts212" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts216">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>. I'm feeling okay with the awkwardness at first, too, right? So there might be some awkwardness, but that's the thing. It takes practice.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a164"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts222" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts224">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Thank you Alex</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
    </TIER>
