        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a156"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts226" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts228">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>SPEAKER_01</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
    </TIER>
    <TIER LINGUISTIC_TYPE_REF="default-lt" TIER_ID="SPEAKER_01">
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a172"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts3" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts7">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Today we're talking about face-to-face communication and why it is so important. And joining me today is Dr. Sarah Tracy. So let's get into the details.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a173"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts9" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts11">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>[music]</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a174"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts15" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts16">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Hey there, welcome back. Today we have a very special guest, Dr. Sarah J. Tracy, a good friend of mine. She's a professor at Arizona State University. She also does speaking and workshops.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a175"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts17" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts20">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>On relationship and connection. So, Sarah.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a176"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts23" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts24">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Thanks for being here. How are you?</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a177"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts31" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts33">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>I'm so glad to have you on. So let's just jump right in. The first question is, there's a lot going on in people's lives today that make the issue of face-to-face conversation that much more important. Can you talk a little bit about what's going on today that makes this so critical?</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a178"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts43" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts44">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yeah, there's a lot of challenges. It seems like we were talking the other day how it's twofold. We're doing less conversation, and so we're losing that time building relationships, but also since we're not doing it, we don't have the skills anymore so that when we actually are face to face, we just don't do it very well. We don't handle it very well.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a179"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts45" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts49">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>So, what's going on today? What are the contributing factors to us losing that ability to connect?</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a180"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts55" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts57">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Right. Yeah, we're just losing that ability. People get so nervous just to have a common conversation, to just ask a stranger a question, like if they're in an unfamiliar part of town or just say hello to them in passing.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a181"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts59" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts61">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>So, we are involved in texting, in social media, clearly, lots of email, and we're on those a lot. And yet, they're not really that good at building these genuine connections. Why is that?</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a182"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts67" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts69">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yeah, you can feel it. You know, if I'm on social media too long, I start to feel that sense of dissatisfaction, like, oh, I'm not going on any good vacations compared to everybody else. You know, my life isn't as good. And it's almost like we might go there, like you say, intending to connect, but then instead we end up pushing out this personal brand. We're marketing ourselves a certain picture, a certain persona that we're pushing out. So I can't really be authentic. I mean, I could in theory, but you don't see that much authenticity. You don't see people having a bad day and using social media to then feel better.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a183"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts75" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts77">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Mm-hmm. Yeah, yeah. Like it if you don't see it, it doesn't exist. And and uh and even if and the other thing is sometimes you'll see someone maybe saying weird things, but you don't you're not sure how to engage with it anyway on social media. Whereas if we were in person, you might sense the moment a little more. You could pick up on small things and then speak into that. Now I've been saying face-to-face conversation. You like the term embodied conversation. Can you tell me what you mean by that?</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a184"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts83" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts85">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>So why is it that that embodied conversation is so important for our well-being?</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a185"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts91" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts93">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yeah, I also enjoy this is a a little off the thing, but when I'm with someone in the room, I can actually see how they react to other people. And so it helps me get to know them in a in a three-dimensional way compared to just receiving a message over email.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a186"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts99" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts101">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>So what are the kinds of issues that are especially important to think about for face-to-face conversation?</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a187"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts107" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts111">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>solve the problem.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a188"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts115" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts117">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yeah, it doesn't work. I d I can't think of a a single conversation over email or text that that that started bad and all of a sudden got better. You know, it seems like we always read into it in exactly the wrong way. But if you're face to face,</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a189"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts119" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts123">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>You can sense their mood a little more. You can kind of tell what's going on. And you hear it, I think, closer to the way they may have intended it.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a190"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts127" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts129">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Right. Tiny little things. They're hard to name, but you can feel it when you're there. So when is it okay? When should we feel okay about using email and text and social media?</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a192"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts135" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts137">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Sure. Sounds like you've been there.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a193"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts140" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts143">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>[laugh]</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a194"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts149" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts153">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yeah, you think why are we even meeting? We could have just put an attachment on an email and this would have been done.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a195"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts157" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts159">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Right. So we're talking about organizations a little bit in here in terms of meetings. And so what can businesses, what can leaders do in the workplace to encourage more face to face conversation?</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a196"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts165" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts167">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Those are great ideas. So I'm going to play the devil's advocate here. Are you saying that bosses, organizations should force people to have face-to-face conversations?</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a197"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts179" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts181">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>I love it. That's so great. You know, there's so much to be said just for being there, just for being available and listening and offering that connection and not having a task, a to-do list, some action items at the end, just to connect and build relationships. And it seems like once you build that relationship, everything else gets easier anyway. So you end up working together more effectively in the long run, anyway. So let's say you're not the boss, you're not a leader, you're not in charge. What can you do personally? What can we do, people watching, do personally to</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a198"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts183" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts185">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>make sure they have more real connection with people.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a199"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts191" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts193">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>This is the email that's gonna change everything.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a200"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts207" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts208">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yeah, it's sometimes those tiny moments, it almost takes a little bit of bravery, but then once the door is open, people are usually quite happy to have a nice conversation, even if it's brief. And then you have a few dozen nice conversations in a day or two, and now you're working in a nice place, right? You enjoy going to work, and you feel like you're part of it.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a201"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts209" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts213">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Enjoy going to work and you feel like you're part of i</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a202"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts219" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts221">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Right, right, great. Well, thank you so much, Sarah, for being here. And just as a reminder for those of you who are watching, you can check out Sarah's content on sarahjtracy.com. She's, as I mentioned, a full professor at Arizona State University. She also has that website where you can find information about her classes, things like syllabi. She's got a blog and other resources as well as a link to her YouTube channel. So Sarah, thank you so much for being on the channel. It's been a real pleasure talking to you.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a203"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts227" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts229">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Thanks everybody and God bless and we'll see you in the next video.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
    </TIER>
    <LINGUISTIC_TYPE GRAPHIC_REFERENCES="false"
        LINGUISTIC_TYPE_ID="default-lt" TIME_ALIGNABLE="true"/>
    <LINGUISTIC_TYPE GRAPHIC_REFERENCES="false"
        LINGUISTIC_TYPE_ID="imported-sep" TIME_ALIGNABLE="true"/>
